 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“The recruitment of Jackie Robinson to the Brooklyn Dodgers in 1947 occurred against the backdrop of the united-front of blacks and whites, leftists and moderates, and labor and civil rights organizations in various parts of the city (New York). Robinson’s recruitment is exemplary of labor civil rights activism over the integration of job sectors – in this case baseball. Although Dodgers owner Branch Rickey recalled the year 1942 as the time when he decided to hire a black player, his recruitment of Robinson occurred later, amid a broad-based community campaign.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">John Hope Franklin, historian, <i>From Slavery to Freedom A History of African Americans 9e,</i> 2011</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Source: McGraw Hill (New York) © 2011, pg. 496</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
